Here's the recommended TypeScript project structure for an Airtable MCP server, following the MCP best practices for stdio-based local servers.

## 1. Directory Layout

```
airtable-mcp/
├── src/
│   ├── index.ts              # Entry point, server setup, transport
│   ├── client.ts             # Airtable API client (auth, fetch wrapper, error handling)
│   ├── tools/
│   │   ├── index.ts          # Registers all tools with the server
│   │   ├── bases.ts          # list_bases, get_base_schema
│   │   ├── records.ts        # list_records, get_record, create_record(s), update_record(s), delete_record(s)
│   │   ├── tables.ts         # create_table, update_table, list_fields
│   │   └── fields.ts         # create_field, update_field
│   ├── types.ts              # Shared TypeScript types (Airtable resource shapes)
│   └── formatting.ts         # Response formatting helpers (JSON/Markdown, pagination)
├── package.json
├── tsconfig.json
├── .env.example               # AIRTABLE_API_KEY placeholder
├── .gitignore
└── README.md
```

Splitting tools by resource type (bases, records, tables, fields) keeps each file small and makes it easy to map to Airtable's REST API sections.

## 2. package.json

```json
{
  "name": "airtable-mcp",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "description": "MCP server for the Airtable API",
  "type": "module",
  "bin": {
    "airtable-mcp": "./build/index.js"
  },
  "files": ["build"],
  "scripts": {
    "build": "tsc && chmod +x build/index.js",
    "watch": "tsc --watch",
    "inspector": "npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector node build/index.js"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk": "^1.0.0",
    "zod": "^3.23.8"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "@types/node": "^20.14.0",
    "typescript": "^5.5.0"
  }
}
```

Note: `chmod +x` in the build script is a no-op on Windows but harmless — keep it for cross-platform npm publishing since it's a no-op error on Windows PowerShell too (npm scripts run via a shell that tolerates it). If you hit issues, drop it and rely on the `bin` shebang alone.

## 3. tsconfig.json

```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "Node16",
    "moduleResolution": "Node16",
    "outDir": "./build",
    "rootDir": "./src",
    "strict": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "declaration": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "build"]
}
```

## 4. Entry point skeleton (`src/index.ts`)

```ts
#!/usr/bin/env node
import { McpServer } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js";
import { StdioServerTransport } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/stdio.js";
import { registerTools } from "./tools/index.js";

const server = new McpServer({
  name: "airtable-mcp",
  version: "0.1.0",
});

registerTools(server);

async function main() {
  const transport = new StdioServerTransport();
  await server.connect(transport);
}

main().catch((err) => {
  console.error("Fatal error:", err);
  process.exit(1);
});
```

## 5. API client skeleton (`src/client.ts`)

```ts
const AIRTABLE_API_BASE = "https://api.airtable.com/v0";

export class AirtableClient {
  constructor(private apiKey: string) {}

  async request<T>(path: string, init: RequestInit = {}): Promise<T> {
    const res = await fetch(`${AIRTABLE_API_BASE}${path}`, {
      ...init,
      headers: {
        Authorization: `Bearer ${this.apiKey}`,
        "Content-Type": "application/json",
        ...init.headers,
      },
    });

    if (!res.ok) {
      const body = await res.text();
      throw new Error(
        `Airtable API error (${res.status}): ${body}. ` +
        `Check that your base/table ID is correct and your API key has the required scopes.`
      );
    }
    return res.json() as Promise<T>;
  }
}
```

Reads `AIRTABLE_API_KEY` from `process.env` at construction time in `index.ts`, so the client itself stays free of env-var lookups.

